import DGPS.Frontier.STR029.T14
import DGPS.Frontier.STR029.T15
import DGPS.Frontier.STR029.T69

/-!
# STR029.16: Unique smooth range graph

Task id: STR029.16 (node STR029, frontier unit).
Title: Unique smooth range graph.

Statement: there are neighbourhoods `U` of zero in `ℝ⁴` and `V_E` of zero in `E` and a smooth
graph `ψ : U → V_E` with `ψ 0 = 0` such that `R(p, w) = 0` in `U × V_E` iff `w = ψ p`. Moreover
`ψ (0, 0, h, γ) = 0` whenever these parameters lie in `U`. The neighbourhoods can be chosen
invariant under the finite group used in STR029.21.

## Provides

* `DGPS.Frontier.STR029.str029_16_unique_smooth_range_graph`: the main statement. There are open
  sets `U ∋ 0` in `ℝ⁴` and `V ∋ 0` in `E` and a map `ψ : ℝ⁴ → E` that is `C^∞` on `U`, with
  `ψ 0 = 0` and `ψ U ⊆ V`, such that for `p ∈ U` and `w ∈ V` one has `R(p, w) = 0 ↔ w = ψ p`.
  Also `ψ (0, 0, h, γ) = 0` whenever `(0, 0, h, γ) ∈ U`. Finally `U` is invariant under the
  rotations, the reflection and the sign map of the parameters and `V` is invariant under all
  pullbacks `w ↦ w (σ · + c)` with `σ = ±1`.

Supporting declarations that later units may reuse (all carry the suffix `_STR02916`):

* `DGPS.Frontier.STR029.implicit_graph_STR02916`: a generic smooth implicit function theorem with
  a common open domain, obtained by applying the Mathlib theorem at every point of the graph.
* `DGPS.Frontier.STR029.Rpar_STR02916`: the range map `R (a, b, h, γ) w` with the four parameters
  grouped as one factor `ℝ⁴`.
* `DGPS.Frontier.STR029.Rpar_equilibrium_STR02916`: `R (0, 0, h, γ) 0 = 0` for all `h` and `γ`.
* `DGPS.Frontier.STR029.pullE_STR02916`, `pullE_val_STR02916`, `norm_pullE_STR02916`: the
  pullback `w ↦ w (σ · + c)` as a self map of `E`, its pointwise formula and the fact that it
  preserves the norm of `E`.
* `DGPS.Frontier.STR029.rotPar_STR02916`, `reflPar_STR02916`, `signPar_STR02916`: the actions on
  the parameters `(a, b, h, γ)` induced by `T_θ`, by `t ↦ -t` and by `T_π` with `h ↦ -h`.

## R5 notes

* No hypothesis is added to the frozen statement. It is stated as one existence claim for
  `(U, V, ψ)` with the listed properties.
* `R` is the variational range map `Rvar_STR02915` of STR029.15, namely `P` applied to the
  gradient `G` of the entropy deficit in the chart `u = a cos t + b sin t + w`. It is not the
  Euler range map, and nothing here assumes the two coincide. The parameters are ordered
  `(a, b, h, γ)`; `Rpar_STR02916 p w` is `Rvar_STR02915 (p.1, p.2.1, p.2.2.1, p.2.2.2, w)`.
* The parameter space `ℝ⁴` is `ℝ × ℝ × ℝ × ℝ` with the product (maximum) norm. `E` is the closed
  subspace of the concrete `C^{2,1/2}` space of STR029.69 cut out by orthogonality to `1`, `cos`
  and `sin`, with the norm `‖w‖∞ + ‖w'‖∞ + ‖w''‖∞ + [w'']_{1/2}` in the circular distance.
  The periodic carriers are those of STR029.68 and STR029.69 and are not rebuilt here.
* "Neighbourhood" is read as an open set containing zero, which is stronger than the wording.
* "Smooth graph `ψ : U → V_E`" is read as: `ψ` is a total function `ℝ⁴ → E`, it is `C^∞` on the
  open set `U` in the sense of `ContDiffOn ℝ ∞`, and `ψ p ∈ V` for every `p ∈ U`. Only the
  restriction of `ψ` to `U` is constrained.
* "`R(p, w) = 0` in `U × V_E` iff `w = ψ p`" is read as: for all `p ∈ U` and `w ∈ V`, the
  equation `R(p, w) = 0` holds exactly when `w = ψ p`.
* The common smooth domain is obtained as in the written proof, not from regularity at zero
  alone: the Mathlib theorem is applied at every graph point of a first graph and the local
  graphs are identified by uniqueness (`implicit_graph_STR02916`). The Neumann series step of
  the sketch is replaced by the openness of the set of invertible bounded operators in Mathlib.
  The inverse of `D_w R(0) = -2 (∂_t² + 1)` is taken from the isomorphism of STR029.14, and its
  norm bound is not used.
* The equilibrium identity `ψ (0, 0, h, γ) = 0` follows from `R (0, 0, h, γ) 0 = 0` (the explicit
  solution `u = 0`, so `G = 0`) together with uniqueness, since `0 ∈ V`.
* The finite group of STR029.21 is read as generated by the translation `T_θ u (t) = u (t - θ)`
  with `θ = 2π/3`, the reflection `t ↦ -t`, and `T_π` combined with `h ↦ -h`. On `E` these are
  the pullbacks `pullE_STR02916 σ _ c` (translation by `θ` is `σ = 1`, `c = -θ`; reflection is
  `σ = -1`, `c = 0`). On the parameters they act by `rotPar_STR02916 θ`, `reflPar_STR02916` and
  `signPar_STR02916`. These actions are defined in this file because the claim of invariance
  needs them; the equivariance of `R` and of `ψ` is the content of STR029.21 and is not claimed.
* Invariance is proved in a stronger form than a finite group requires: `U` is a Euclidean ball
  of `ℝ⁴`, invariant under `rotPar_STR02916 θ` for every real `θ`, and `V` is a norm ball of
  `E`, invariant under `pullE_STR02916 σ _ c` for every real `c` and `σ = ±1` (these maps
  preserve the norm of `E`). Invariance is stated as `g U ⊆ U` and `g V ⊆ V`; each group
  element has its inverse in the same family, so this is equality of sets.
* Sizes are shrunk in the order: the Mathlib neighbourhoods give an open parameter set `U₀` and
  an open set `N₀` of `E`; `V` is a ball in `N₀`; continuity of the graph at zero gives a ball of
  `ℝ⁴` mapped into `V`; `U` is the Euclidean ball of the same radius inside it.
* The angular variable `t` is the periodic variable with normalized measure `dt/(2π)`. The
  optimizer-chart predicate, the weighted carriers, the AbsoluteDensity domain and the entropy,
  Fisher and deficit conventions enter only through the gradient of STR029.50 through STR029.55,
  as used by STR029.15. No angular-to-physical conversion enters this unit.
-/

